WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:10.240
RNA-Seq data are used to study which genes in an&nbsp;
organism are expressed under different conditions.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:15.920
RNA-Seq data also provide an estimate of relative&nbsp;
gene expression levels within a single sample.

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:22.560
However, when RNA-Seq data are initially obtained,&nbsp;
the data are simply a collection of sequence

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:29.440
information with no genomic context. We do not&nbsp;
know which genes are expressed and at what levels.

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:35.040
In this video, we will discuss the computational&nbsp;
approaches that enable RNA-Seq data to be

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:41.120
efficiently mapped to an organism's genome&nbsp;
assembly. In order to produce RNA-seq data, we

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:47.520
need to first obtain and sequence the RNA from the&nbsp;
organism we are studying. RNA can be extracted from

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:53.360
an organism at specific developmental stages from&nbsp;
specific tissues or after different treatments.

00:00:54.080 --> 00:01:00.320
The extracted RNAs undergo library preparation&nbsp;
and are most often sequenced using the Illumina

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:06.320
sequencing technology. We call the sequence data&nbsp;
obtained from sequencing a fragment of RNA a "read".

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:13.520
In general, reads produced by the Illumina&nbsp;
sequencers are between 75 and 150 bases long.

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:20.400
The resulting RNA-Seq data allow us to identify&nbsp;
genes that are expressed and to identify genes

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:25.680
that show changes in expression levels under&nbsp;
different conditions. After the sequence data

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:31.360
have been obtained, we need to map the RNA-Seq&nbsp;
reads to the genome so that we can determine

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:37.840
the genes that are being expressed. This is a&nbsp;
difficult computational problem. The sizes of the

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:43.760
genome assembly for eukaryotes can range from&nbsp;
tens of millions to tens of billions of bases,

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:48.320
and the Illumina sequencer can produce&nbsp;
hundreds of millions of RNA-Seq reads.

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:54.480
In addition, because of sequencing errors and&nbsp;
polymorphisms, we need to allow for a small number

00:01:54.480 --> 00:02:00.800
of differences between the RNA-Seq reads and the&nbsp;
genome assembly. If we were to use an algorithm

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:06.560
like BLAST to perform this search, the process&nbsp;
would be extremely slow because the amount of

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:12.000
time required to search each RNA-Seq read would&nbsp;
be proportional to the length of the database.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:17.760
Hence, we must use an alternative approach that&nbsp;
reduces the amount of time required to search for

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:24.400
sequence similarity between each RNA read and the&nbsp;
genome assembly. This alternative approach takes

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:31.200
advantage of a data structure in computer science&nbsp;
known as a suffix tree. The suffix tree creates an

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:37.520
index of the genome assembly, thereby allowing us&nbsp;
to quickly find the locations where the RNA-seq

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:43.520
read matches the genome assembly. We will use a&nbsp;
simple example to illustrate how to construct a

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:50.960
suffix tree. Our database consists of a sequence of&nbsp;
DNA with 10 nucleotides. We will first specify the

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:57.600
position of each base in the sequence starting&nbsp;
from position 0. The dollar sign at position 10

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:03.680
denotes the end of the sequence. To help us&nbsp;
construct a suffix tree, we will first create

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:10.800
a suffix array. Each element in the suffix array&nbsp;
corresponds to a position in the DNA sequence and

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:19.520
its corresponding suffix. For example, at position&nbsp;
10, the suffix is the dollar sign. At position 9,

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:27.440
the suffix is an "a" followed by the dollar sign.&nbsp;
The suffix "ga$" is at position 8.

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:34.880
The suffix "aga$" is at position&nbsp;
7 and so on. We can continue to build this

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:42.400
list backwards until we reach position 0, where the&nbsp;
suffix is the entire DNA sequence in the database.

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:49.360
We then sort these suffixes alphabetically while&nbsp;
also keeping track of the position of each suffix.

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:56.160
We can convert a suffix array into a suffix&nbsp;
tree by merging the common suffixes in the

00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:02.720
list of sorted suffixes. We will examine each&nbsp;
column of the suffix array and create a separate

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:09.760
branch for each unique nucleotide. For example,&nbsp;
in the first column of the suffix array we have

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:18.480
the nucleotides "a," "c," and "g." Hence, we will create&nbsp;
three branches in the first level of the tree.

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:25.200
For the subset of suffixes that begin with an&nbsp;
"a," the second column consists of the dollar sign,

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:31.200
"c," and "g." This means we will create three&nbsp;
branches at the second level of the tree.

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:37.200
When we reach the dollar sign, we will record&nbsp;
the position in the leaf of the tree. In this

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:44.240
case, we will record the position 9 for the&nbsp;
suffix "a$." We can repeat this process

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:48.720
to construct the rest of the subtree&nbsp;
for the suffixes that begin with an "a."

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:54.960
Similarly, we can repeat this process to construct&nbsp;
the subtrees for the suffixes that begin with a

00:04:54.960 --> 00:05:02.400
"c" or a "g." After we have created the suffix tree, it&nbsp;
now becomes much easier to find all the matches to

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:09.520
a read in the database. For example, if we want to&nbsp;
find the sequence "cag" in our database we simply

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:15.680
follow the branches of the tree until we match the&nbsp;
entire read and report the positions in the leaves:

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:21.680
in this case, positions 1 and 6. The amount of&nbsp;
time required to find all matches to a read

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:26.720
is proportional to the logarithm of the length&nbsp;
of the read and does not depend on the size of

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:34.000
the database. We can modify the search algorithm to&nbsp;
allow inexact matches. To allow base substitutions,

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:39.520
we can follow multiple paths but charge a penalty&nbsp;
for each mismatch. We can terminate the search

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:45.920
within a subtree once the number of mismatches&nbsp;
exceeds the maximum number of differences allowed.

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:52.080
To facilitate inexact matches with insertions&nbsp;
and deletions, we can use dynamic programming or

00:05:52.080 --> 00:06:02.640
a heuristic search to compute the edit distance&nbsp;
of the read against many paths in the suffix tree.

